---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Layers', href: '#layers' },
  { label: 'Rust and adapter fixtures', href: '#rust' },
  { label: 'Fake harness', href: '#harness' },
  { label: 'UI surfaces', href: '#ui' },
  { label: 'Live smokes', href: '#live' },
  { label: 'Docs tests', href: '#docs' },
  { label: 'Rules', href: '#rules' },
]

const commands = `direnv exec . cargo test -p agent-harness
direnv exec . cargo test -p shepherd-core
direnv exec . pnpm --dir apps/shepherd/ui run test:e2e
pnpm --dir apps/shep-docs-web run test:e2e`
---

<Docs
  title="Test fixtures"
  description="The fixture layers that keep adapter, terminal, preview and docs behavior testable without a live provider."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    Shepherd tests behavior at the boundary that owns it. Protocol parsing is tested with recorded
    fixtures; turn assembly with a fake harness; the shell with a Playwright webview; and installed
    agents only in explicitly gated live smokes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layers">Layers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unit and contract tests</strong> — pure parsing, validation, Git, store and settings behavior.</li>
    <li><strong>Fake adapters</strong> — drive the core's event and approval contract without a provider.</li>
    <li><strong>Playwright shell tests</strong> — the Tauri webview's palette, task workspace, terminals, preview and settings flows.</li>
    <li><strong>Live smokes</strong> — opt-in probes against installed CLIs, disabled by default and never the only coverage for a feature.</li>
    <li><strong>Docs tests</strong> — route, navigation, responsive overflow and accessibility checks for this site.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rust">Rust and adapter fixtures</h2>
  <p>
    Native adapters use recorded protocol fixtures for parsing and event mapping. The ACP fake agent
    is built under <code>test-support</code> and covers filesystem, terminal and permission requests.
    Terminal-wrapper manifests are tested against captured screen output.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each adapter has explicit failure fixtures: binary missing, version too old, auth required and
    process exit mid-turn. Native RPC fixtures cover framing and record-family probing; a missing
    approval bridge must surface an unreviewed-tools state rather than a fake approval.
  </p>

  <h2 id="harness">Fake harness</h2>
  <p>
    The harness contract test drives <code>TurnStarted</code>, <code>Delta</code>,
    <code>ToolCall</code> and <code>TurnFinished</code>; approval requests with and without a patch;
    and a process exit without a finish. The core must not infer completion. Out-of-order updates must
    map to one canonical entry, and ordering must survive a reconnect.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use temporary repositories and disposable data directories for persistence, worktree and settings
    tests. A test must not touch the developer's real project list or database.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ui">UI surfaces</h2>
  <p>
    The v1 Playwright suite runs against the Tauri webview. It covers the palette, task list, project
    switching, fixed chat/inspector regions, shared and task terminals, review attention, preview
    element annotation and the screenshot fallback. The native macOS smokes cover window focus,
    visibility and IME behavior that a headless browser cannot.
  </p>
  <p>
    Terminal tests assert parser and frame state, not pixels. Git tests use temporary repositories for
    status, diffs, hooks, signing disabled, worktrees, dirty refusal and squash-merge conflicts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="live">Live smokes</h2>
  <p>
    Installed-process probes are excluded by default and behind a per-agent feature. A replay would
    skip the process being tested, so a cache miss needs the feature rather than a silent pass.
    Codex scenarios replay app-server responses and recorded worktree edits under
    <code>.cache/shepherd-live/codex/terra-low-v1/</code>. Recording uses
    <code>gpt-6.1-terra</code> at <code>low</code> effort; captures with another model or effort, or
    an empty session, are rejected.
  </p>
  <p>
    The adapter smoke workflow chooses one installed agent on a self-hosted runner. Do not launch a
    real agent in a test that is supposed to use a fixture, and never put credentials or tokens in a
    fixture.
  </p>

  <h2 id="docs">Docs tests</h2>
  <p>
    This package's Playwright suite runs the built site on its own preview port. It checks every
    user and contributor route, the guide tabs, the shared navigation, responsive overflow at 375,
    768 and 1440 px, and axe WCAG 2.1 AA. It also fails on an internal reference-project name or a
    planned feature described as available.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={commands} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    The committed <code>test:e2e</code> scripts clean stale preview state before and after the run;
    do not add manual port or worker flags to the documented command.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rules">Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A test that needs a real CLI must be gated and documented; the default suite uses fixtures.</li>
    <li>Never assert a provider's live quota or model output in a deterministic test.</li>
    <li>Keep tokens, claims, provider keys and launch-environment values out of fixtures, logs and screenshots.</li>
    <li>Run the narrowest suite that covers the change, then the package's committed typecheck and e2e script before handing it back.</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout tone="note" title="A failure is not a flake by default">
    Fix the cause. Do not skip a test, weaken an assertion or add a retry to hide a real race. If the
    environment genuinely cannot run a probe, report it as a limitation.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
